---
title: Penduline tit
---
# Penduline tit

The penduline tit is a small, masked songbird of reedbeds and riverside willows
across Europe and western Asia. The male slings a thick-walled purse for the
eggs from a drooping twig, felting seed down, willow catkin and spider silk into
a fabric strong enough to hold a hand's weight, and finishes it with a
downward-facing entrance tube. Nothing supports the purse from below: it swings
free at the tip of the twig, and a false entrance beside the real one leads to a
blind pocket that may delay a predator. Pairings are brief and either sex may
desert, so the purse is often finished by one parent alone. See [[remizidae]].
